WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.151
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:06.451 --> 00:00:09.896
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.196 --> 00:00:17.411
Unit eighteen: Media Literacy. Today you will explain cause and effect with because of, due to and as a result.

4
00:00:17.711 --> 00:00:20.247
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.502
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:26.061
Bias.

7
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.769
Bias means an unfair preference for one side.

8
00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:32.685
Bias can hide in the choice of words.

9
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:36.052
Your turn. Say: Bias.

10
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:39.831
Bias.

11
00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:42.095
Source credibility.

12
00:00:43.083 --> 00:00:46.523
Source credibility means how far a source can be trusted.

13
00:00:47.117 --> 00:00:49.592
Source credibility depends on evidence.

14
00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:53.602
Your turn. Say: Source credibility.

15
00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:57.967
Source credibility.

16
00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:00.101
Media ownership.

17
00:01:01.117 --> 00:01:04.881
Media ownership means who owns and controls a news company.

18
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:07.785
Media ownership may shape the coverage.

19
00:01:08.383 --> 00:01:11.500
Your turn. Say: Media ownership.

20
00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:15.786
Media ownership.

21
00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.027
Sponsored content.

22
00:01:19.017 --> 00:01:22.793
Sponsored content means paid advertising made to look like news.

23
00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:26.091
Sponsored content should be clearly labelled.

24
00:01:26.683 --> 00:01:29.847
Your turn. Say: Sponsored content.

25
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.131
Sponsored content.

26
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:36.047
Algorithm.

27
00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.930
Algorithm means a set of rules a computer uses to choose what you see.

28
00:01:41.517 --> 00:01:44.364
An algorithm decides which videos you see.

29
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.776
Your turn. Say: Algorithm.

30
00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:51.634
Algorithm.

31
00:01:52.383 --> 00:01:53.501
Echo chamber.

32
00:01:54.517 --> 00:01:58.757
Echo chamber means a place where you only hear opinions like your own.

33
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.976
Social media can become an echo chamber.

34
00:02:02.583 --> 00:02:05.665
Your turn. Say: Echo chamber.

35
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:09.745
Echo chamber.

36
00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:11.775
Critical reading.

37
00:02:12.783 --> 00:02:16.141
Critical reading means reading carefully and questioning what you read.

38
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:19.017
Critical reading protects us from rumours.

39
00:02:19.617 --> 00:02:22.339
Your turn. Say: Critical reading.

40
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.526
Critical reading.

41
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:28.656
Diverse sources.

42
00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:33.612
Diverse sources means information from many different people and outlets.

43
00:02:34.217 --> 00:02:36.599
Diverse sources give a fuller picture.

44
00:02:37.183 --> 00:02:40.590
Your turn. Say: Diverse sources.

45
00:02:43.433 --> 00:02:44.690
Diverse sources.

46
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:49.371
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:50.217 --> 00:02:53.865
Visal, why do I see the same opinions on my feed every day?

48
00:02:54.950 --> 00:03:02.699
Because of the algorithm. It shows you what you already like, and as a result you may live in an echo chamber.

49
00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:05.202
How can I escape it?

50
00:03:06.317 --> 00:03:16.167
Practise critical reading and use diverse sources. Due to media ownership and bias, one outlet alone can give a limited view.

51
00:03:17.283 --> 00:03:19.527
Should I check who pays for a story?

52
00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:27.263
Yes. Sponsored content looks like news, so ask about source credibility every time.

53
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.063
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:30.917 --> 00:03:34.925
Grammar focus: Cause and effect: because of and due to and as a result.

55
00:03:35.225 --> 00:03:36.423
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:40.382
Because of the algorithm, we see similar opinions.

57
00:03:41.283 --> 00:03:43.991
Bias is often due to media ownership.

58
00:03:44.883 --> 00:03:48.755
We read one outlet. As a result, our view becomes narrow.

59
00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:57.057
Because of and due to are followed by a noun. As a result starts a new sentence with a comma. Do not use 'because of' before a clause.

60
00:03:57.817 --> 00:04:02.048
Be careful! Don't say: Because of he lied, we doubt him.

61
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.418
Say: Because of his lies, we doubt him.

62
00:04:06.718 --> 00:04:08.659
Because of is followed by a noun.

63
00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:12.386
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.619
Reading Beyond the Feed.

65
00:04:14.919 --> 00:04:18.660
Every day, an algorithm chooses which stories appear on a phone.

66
00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:24.162
Because of this, many readers sit in an echo chamber and see only familiar opinions.

67
00:04:25.167 --> 00:04:30.858
Media ownership and sponsored content can also create bias, which is why source credibility matters.

68
00:04:31.867 --> 00:04:36.548
Critical reading and diverse sources help readers compare views and avoid being misled.

69
00:04:37.533 --> 00:04:40.787
As a result, informed citizens make better decisions.

70
00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:45.867
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:46.717 --> 00:04:49.494
blank the algorithm, we see similar opinions.

72
00:04:49.794 --> 00:04:54.051
A, Because of. B, Because. C, As a result.

73
00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.611
The answer is A: Because of.

74
00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:04.050
A place where you only hear opinions like your own is an blank.

75
00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:08.131
A, echo chamber. B, algorithm. C, outlet.

76
00:05:11.667 --> 00:05:14.165
The answer is A: echo chamber.

77
00:05:15.317 --> 00:05:18.454
Paid advertising made to look like news is blank content.

78
00:05:18.754 --> 00:05:22.454
A, diverse. B, sponsored. C, critical.

79
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.104
The answer is B: sponsored.

80
00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.006
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:31.306 --> 00:05:38.303
A, Because of he is biased, we doubt him.. B, Because of his bias, we doubt him.. C, Because his bias of, we doubt him..

82
00:05:41.867 --> 00:05:45.387
The answer is B: Because of his bias, we doubt him..

83
00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:49.652
According to the reading, diverse sources help readers…

84
00:05:49.952 --> 00:05:54.244
A, compare views. B, avoid reading. C, buy papers.

85
00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.032
The answer is A: compare views.

86
00:06:01.233 --> 00:06:04.257
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:05.117 --> 00:06:08.962
How can reading news from diverse sources help you avoid an echo chamber?

88
00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:19.320
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:39.170
Reading diverse sources shows me opinions that my feed would normally hide. If I compare two or three outlets, I can notice bias and missing facts. As a result, I understand an issue more fully and can form my own view. It also keeps me open to people who think differently.

90
00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:41.121
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:42.017 --> 00:06:48.129
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and cause and effect: because of and due to and as a result.

92
00:06:48.429 --> 00:06:53.229
Today's value is open-mindedness. Listen to new ideas before you judge them.

93
00:06:53.529 --> 00:06:56.181
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:56.481 --> 00:06:59.122
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

